<h2>When to use what</h2>
<p>Use Pilot Protocol when:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Building with AI agents that need to find, trust, and communicate with each other.</li>
  <li>A lightweight network is needed with no accounts, PKI, or cloud platform to manage.</li>
  <li>Built-in application services like data exchange and pub/sub are required.</li>
  <li>Agents need to dynamically discover peers by tags, hostnames, or capabilities.</li>
  <li>Agents need to negotiate trust at runtime without a central authority.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use Tailscale when:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A VPN mesh is needed for human users and their devices.</li>
  <li>SSO integration (Google, Microsoft, Okta) is required.</li>
  <li>Centralized access control managed by an admin is required.</li>
  <li>An admin console and commercial support are desired.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use ZeroTier when:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A flat L2 network is needed for up to 25 devices on the free tier.</li>
  <li>Virtual Ethernet between devices across the internet is desired.</li>
  <li>Broad platform support is required.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use Nebula when:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Connecting servers in a known, static infrastructure.</li>
  <li>A PKI is already in place or running a certificate authority is acceptable.</li>
  <li>Fine-grained firewall rules defined in config files are needed.</li>
  <li>MIT-licensed software with proven scale is desired.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use libp2p when:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Building a blockchain, decentralized storage, or Web3 application.</li>
  <li>Maximum protocol flexibility and transport agnosticism are needed.</li>
  <li>DHT-based fully decentralized discovery is desired.</li>
  <li>Time can be invested in assembling and configuring the stack.</li>
</ul>
<p>Tailscale, ZeroTier, and Nebula provide a network. Pilot Protocol provides agents a network with identity, trust, discovery, and services built in. For human or server nodes, use a VPN. For agent nodes, use Pilot.</p>

